#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"}] :question/id #uuid "671da5f4-e539-588e-9bec-81c71e016d2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5104" :question/problem "In a class, each student selected one course among the two possible options, math or physics. The results are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Math | Physics | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $12$ | $12$ | $24$ |\n| Male | $20$ | $6$ | $26$ |\n| Totals | $32$ | $18$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student selected physics or is male (or both)." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student selected physics or is male, we\n\n- add up the numbers that are in the column **\"Physics\"** or the row **\"Male\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the column **\"Physics\"** or the row **\"Male\"** are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Math | Physics | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $12$ | $\\color{blue}12$ | $24$ |\n| Male | $\\color{blue}20$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $26$ |\n| Totals | $32$ | $18$ | $50$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50),$ we get\n\n$$\np =\\dfrac{12+20+6}{50} = \\dfrac{38}{50} =0.76.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054552 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054662 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46546a09-5d48-57d9-8166-5f9d8949c066" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Intersection of Events"}] :question/id #uuid "ec0a90ec-7cd7-5ab5-abb2-9e33cbd4b711" :question/math-academy-id "e-5105" :question/problem "Anna surveys some students in a school, asking them what pets out of cats or dogs they have at home. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below. \n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $30$ | $56$ | $86$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $60$ | $54$ | $114$ |\n| Totals | $90$ | $110$ | $200$ |\n\nLet $C$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the school **\"Has a cat\"**, and $D$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the school **\"Has a dog\"**. Estimate $P(C \\cap D).$" :question/worked-solution "The event $C \\cap D$ is the event that a randomly selected student in the school has both a cat and a dog.\n\nTo estimate the probability of $C \\cap D,$ we need to \n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a cat\"** and the column **\"Has a dog\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a cat\"** and the column **\"Has a dog\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $\\color{blue}30$ | $56$ | $86$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $60$ | $54$ | $114$ |\n| Totals | $90$ | $110$ | $200$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(200),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(C \\cap D) = \\dfrac{30}{200} = 0.15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054553 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054663 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating Using Probabilities"}] :question/id #uuid "f09f68d2-f44f-5328-963b-41a9884a944a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5106" :question/problem "There are $250$ boys and $250$ girls in a school. Of them, $40$ boys and $40$ girls were randomly selected and asked which sport they prefer between volleyball, basketball, football, or soccer. The results of the survey are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Volleyball | Basketball | Football | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girl | $14$ | $10$ | $2$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Boy | $8$ | $12$ | $11$ | $9$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $22$ | $22$ | $13$ | $23$ | $80$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly selected student in the survey is a boy who prefers football or soccer, and then use this to estimate the number of students in the entire school who are boys that prefer football or soccer." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected student is a boy who prefers football or soccer, we\n\n- add up the numbers at the intersection of the row **\"Boy\"** and the columns **\"Football\"** and **\"Soccer\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers at the intersection of the row **\"Boy\"** and the columns **\"Football\"** and **\"Soccer\"** are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Volleyball | Basketball | Football | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girl | $14$ | $10$ | $2$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Boy | $8$ | $12$ | $\\color{blue}11$ | $\\color{blue}9$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $22$ | $22$ | $13$ | $23$ | $80$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(80),$ we get\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{11+9}{80} = \\dfrac{20}{80} = 0.25.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student is a boy who prefers football or soccer.\n\nFinally, to estimate the number of students in the entire school who are boys that prefer football or soccer, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(500)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =500\\cdot p \\\\  & =500\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =125\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054662 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46546a09-5d48-57d9-8166-5f9d8949c066" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Intersection of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190d1822-15e6-544f-9385-1f1a371a7f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559073} {:answer/id #uuid "29d873cd-bd5d-5487-a5cc-6d16874747e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559121} {:answer/id #uuid "80f1cd68-0360-542c-a764-5f25fc1ae4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559337} {:answer/id #uuid "a69a67f1-d67f-55f6-bc86-fe6931a33ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559435} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb6c481-65fa-5471-b76a-3501659d2594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "190d1822-15e6-544f-9385-1f1a371a7f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559073} :answer-field/id #uuid "9973b090-3528-53c7-bd3d-78df1d7984eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4bda52d-6631-58b0-bd68-5066bd3ff243" :question/math-academy-id "q-120213" :question/problem "A survey asks a group of students about their eye color. The findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Green | Blue | Brown | Black | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $12$ | $8$ | $10$ | $40$ |\n| Boys | $13$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $60$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $24$ | $23$ | $30$ | $100$ |\n\nLet $B$ be the event that a randomly selected student is a **\"Boy\"**, and $C$ be the event that a randomly selected student has **\"Brown\"** eyes. Estimate $P(B\\cap C).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $B\\cap C$ is the event that a randomly selected student is a boy and has brown eyes. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $B\\cap C,$ we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Brown\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Boys\"** and the column **\"Brown\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Green | Blue | Brown | Black | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $12$ | $8$ | $10$ | $40$ |\n| Boys | $13$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $60$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $24$ | $23$ | $30$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(B\\cap C)=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a49678d-d723-5baf-a3f7-85df13a9862d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559123} {:answer/id #uuid "7171b77b-3a95-5010-8fc6-ab0b9e4f572b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559297} {:answer/id #uuid "798f5e79-d365-55c8-b7b7-a04ca9d22c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559320} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2c18ee-e0db-54f6-a707-3ed8f48a6869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559330} {:answer/id #uuid "98f4c760-235f-57e5-970c-cc4ebdf54e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7171b77b-3a95-5010-8fc6-ab0b9e4f572b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559297} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1c7db9-a63f-5475-be38-0bf2636d125e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c991f61-1cd6-5a73-ae3c-f2cc1cf3d784" :question/math-academy-id "q-120355" :question/problem "Some customers in a pizzeria were asked about the type of pizza they prefer between spicy pizza and vegetarian pizza. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Prefers Spicy | Prefers  Vegetarian | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Children | $10$ | $22$ | $32$ |\n| Adults | $41$ | $27$ | $68$ |\n| Totals | $51$ | $49$ | $100$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen person is an adult or prefers spicy pizza (or both)." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen person is an adult or prefers spicy pizza, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Adults\"** or the column **\"Prefers Spicy\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the row **\"Adults\"** or the column **\"Prefers Spicy\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Prefers Spicy | Prefers Vegetarian | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Children | $10$ | $22$ | $32$ |\n| Adults | $41$ | $27$ | $68$ |\n| Totals | $51$ | $49$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{10+41+27}{100}=\\frac{78}{100}=0.78.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054663 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating Using Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcb8eb2-1560-57cf-a874-7ed572879682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559081} {:answer/id #uuid "2488f5ae-cfa6-5394-80ad-3eda46d13152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559106} {:answer/id #uuid "314daf73-8d31-5f75-8059-c0cc9efa2cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559142} {:answer/id #uuid "42cbdb5d-db43-53b8-9021-b975a8784198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559189} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6ac013-5d00-5a8c-8c3b-61dfe2fc8fac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2488f5ae-cfa6-5394-80ad-3eda46d13152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559106} :answer-field/id #uuid "563810e7-f31e-5609-8eea-711388584e00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d65fd340-5d3c-5485-87ce-64ffa546bd48" :question/math-academy-id "q-120744" :question/problem "A group of randomly selected customers from a certain insurance company was asked about the type of vehicle driven and whether they received a speeding ticket in the last year. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | SUV | Sport car | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Speeding ticket | $80$ | $5$ | $85$ |\n| No speeding ticket | $20$ | $5$ | $25$ |\n| Totals | $100$ | $10$ | $110$ |\n\nThere are $220$ drivers in another insurance company. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same for both companies, estimate the number of drivers in the second insurance company that drive SUV or received a speeding ticket, but not both." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected driver drives the SUV or received a speeding ticket but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Speeding ticket\"** or the column **\"SUV\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | SUV | Sport car | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Speeding ticket | $80$ | $5$ | $85$ |\n| No speeding ticket | $20$ | $5$ | $25$ |\n| Totals | $100$ | $10$ | $110$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(110),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{20+5}{110}=\\frac{5}{22}.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected driver drives an SUV or received a speeding ticket but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of drivers in the second company that drive SUVs or received a speeding ticket, but not both, we multiply the total number of drivers in the company $(220)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =220\\cdot p \\\\  & =220\\cdot \\frac{5}{22} \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07424aae-1a8f-5e91-8cc7-dc60fa630f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559031} {:answer/id #uuid "37304a72-8df6-5b71-9b1f-3f7ac729e3cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559156} {:answer/id #uuid "8153d023-9387-553c-9617-5934a186fac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559339} {:answer/id #uuid "dafcf6b4-a9e9-55f2-97fa-90ca94c757ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559554} {:answer/id #uuid "f40875d7-a2ed-54b4-a8e6-34d0984f8575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07424aae-1a8f-5e91-8cc7-dc60fa630f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559031} :answer-field/id #uuid "22e13ec8-77ce-5832-bf60-b058ef11f3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cca7bbd7-fb2c-50b2-babf-c466777230b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-46814" :question/problem "Martin surveyed some students in a school, asking them if they have cats or dogs at home. His findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\" Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the row **\"Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(32),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9+10}{32}=\\frac{19}{32}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f5bd6d2-d38a-5a10-8544-11a2462b35d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559047} {:answer/id #uuid "21106799-78c1-5b12-97b2-81a10265b2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559094} {:answer/id #uuid "621d3fb4-01e5-586d-9831-a425cefbd44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.79" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559262} {:answer/id #uuid "877aa175-6eda-5e56-a7fa-927dab2bcc2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559353} {:answer/id #uuid "d9548fc2-50fc-5324-992f-44af5d618220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21106799-78c1-5b12-97b2-81a10265b2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559094} :answer-field/id #uuid "84661f42-becc-5c57-a165-fc16451678cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9952b961-280f-5d1f-a0eb-bf2ca705ef14" :question/math-academy-id "q-47137" :question/problem "A randomly selected group of students from a high school were asked about their favorite sport between baseball, football, basketball, or soccer. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Baseball | Football | Basketball | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $19$ | $10$ | $12$ | $51$ |\n| Boys | $10$ | $13$ | $20$ | $6$ | $49$ |\n| Totals | $20$ | $32$ | $30$ | $18$ | $100$ |\n\nLet $F$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the high school is a **\"Girl\"** whose favorite sport is **\"Football\"**, and $S$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the high school is a **\"Girl\"** whose favorite sport is **\"Soccer\"**. Estimate $P(F\\cup S).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $F\\cup S$ is the event that a randomly selected student in the high school is a girl whose favorite sport is football or a girl whose favorite sport is soccer. \n\nTo estimate the probability of $F\\cup S,$ we\n\n- add up the numbers in the columns **\"Football\"** or **\"Soccer\"** and the row **\"Girls\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the columns **\"Football\"** or **\"Soccer\"** and the row **\"Girls\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Baseball | Football | Basketball | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $10$ | $19$ | $10$ | $12$ | $51$ |\n| Boys | $10$ | $13$ | $20$ | $6$ | $49$ |\n| Totals | $20$ | $32$ | $30$ | $18$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100),$ we get\n\n$$\nP(F\\cup S)=\\frac{19+12}{100}=\\frac{31}{100}=0.31.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054663 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating Using Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af2f72a0-8f30-5a0e-a022-a97a7f52f774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053212} {:answer/id #uuid "d1817f58-64f5-546d-a957-b253a7f8da60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053213} {:answer/id #uuid "fe64e4fa-548b-5090-aefd-9533e1e17236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053214} {:answer/id #uuid "1af1780d-1f06-5e56-81bc-bfccd4223b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053215} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd3bbf4-e126-5de2-9d39-f96423983718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1af1780d-1f06-5e56-81bc-bfccd4223b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053215} :answer-field/id #uuid "16caa5bd-0c6c-52c9-be37-956a1d8a8e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa1f968b-ce45-555d-acfc-8866cb45d38d" :question/math-academy-id "q-47138" :question/problem "A group of randomly selected students from School A was asked about whether or not they use TV or the internet to access the local news. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | TV | No TV | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Internet | $90$ | $15$ | $105$ |\n| No internet | $30$ | $15$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $120$ | $30$ | $150$ |\n\nThere are $100$ students in School B. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same for both schools, \nestimate the number of School B students that access the local news either from TV or the internet, but not both." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected student gets the local news either from TV or the internet but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"No internet\"** or the column **\"TV\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | TV | No TV | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Internet | $90$ | $15$ | $105$ |\n| No internet | $30$ | $15$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $120$ | $30$ | $150$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(150),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{30+15}{150}=\\frac{45}{150}=0.3.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student gets the local news from TV or the internet but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of students of School B that get their local news either from TV or the internet but not both, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(100)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =100\\cdot p \\\\  & =100\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f5c3b02-3757-5ac4-8689-9a0642cbbf9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053224} {:answer/id #uuid "91c02db5-84de-5acc-abef-97f62ab3cd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053225} {:answer/id #uuid "92959582-544d-51a2-9941-734879b6e119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053226} {:answer/id #uuid "27190c4d-66f9-539b-b083-e122239eba9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053227} {:answer/id #uuid "5434afcf-de9a-5ac6-a3f1-0a118a94fca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92959582-544d-51a2-9941-734879b6e119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053226} :answer-field/id #uuid "baeac06b-4ef9-51f2-94b1-0894733ab9a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66372d89-3abf-597d-98d0-70064323b74c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47141" :question/problem "In a high school, a group of students was asked about whether they prefer apples, oranges, or bananas. The results are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Apple | Orange | Banana | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $11$ | $8$ | $7$ | $26$ |\n| Boys | $12$ | $6$ | $6$ | $24$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $14$ | $13$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the group preferred apples or bananas or is a girl." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student preferred apples or bananas or is a girl, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the columns **\"Apple\"** or **\"Banana\"** or the row **\"Girls\"**, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the columns **\"Apple\"** or **\"Banana\"** or the row **\"Girls\"** are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Apple | Orange | Banana | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $11$ | $8$ | $7$ | $26$ |\n| Boys | $12$ | $6$ | $6$ | $24$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $14$ | $13$ | $50$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{11+12+8+7+6}{50}=\\frac{44}{50}=0.88.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054663 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating Using Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9ae188-7070-59ce-83f7-d90031a412cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "205" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559079} {:answer/id #uuid "23b47667-2432-502e-9d89-0418efac7dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "230" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559103} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab12cea-17ac-5923-b8f1-01577e5c7cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559365} {:answer/id #uuid "9ddef87b-6f35-5758-8f4e-c95aa2e3bf37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559417} {:answer/id #uuid "b86630e8-ba9b-59bc-8cc8-16a010d23bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "218" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b86630e8-ba9b-59bc-8cc8-16a010d23bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "218" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559476} :answer-field/id #uuid "1883e150-ff1f-5b5f-b3d9-307772697e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7775a984-c5ac-5540-8a1b-b2aae6fb0655" :question/math-academy-id "q-47294" :question/problem "Roger surveyed $62$ Jefferson High School students. He asked them if they like biology and chemistry. His findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Like biology | Doesn't like biology | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Like chemistry | $25$ | $19$ | $44$ |\n| Doesn't like chemistry | $8$ | $10$ | $18$ |\n| Totals | $33$ | $29$ | $62$ |\n\nEdison High School has $500$ students. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same in both high schools, determine the number of Edison High School students who like biology or chemistry but not both. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the number of students who like biology or chemistry but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Like chemistry\"** or the column **\" Like biology\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | Like biology | Doesn't like biology | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Like chemistry | $25$ | $19$ | $44$ |\n| Doesn't like chemistry | $8$ | $10$ | $18$ |\n| Totals | $33$ | $29$ | $62$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(62),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8+19}{62}=\\frac{27}{62}=0.435.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student likes biology or chemistry but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of students of Edison High School who like biology or chemistry but not both, we multiply the total number of students in the Edison High School $(500)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =500\\cdot p \\\\  & =500\\cdot 0.435 \\\\  & =217.5 \\\\  & =218,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054662 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46546a09-5d48-57d9-8166-5f9d8949c066" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Intersection of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57a0e54d-4f68-52ef-bcd6-35fdedddd325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559242} {:answer/id #uuid "66c02dd9-17da-5354-94ef-38cd224eab8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559270} {:answer/id #uuid "66fed658-14bf-5a34-8fa4-66c8e5cd9b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559272} {:answer/id #uuid "d026f55c-173b-52ab-8488-49e2ea0ff410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559519} {:answer/id #uuid "e01bbae9-b081-583d-8d6f-1fd6591bc0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57a0e54d-4f68-52ef-bcd6-35fdedddd325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559242} :answer-field/id #uuid "0433ce33-a4a9-5267-807e-305c8929c939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e30d37ae-3013-5226-8257-f893cef40757" :question/math-academy-id "q-74424" :question/problem "Dana surveys $72$ randomly chosen students at her school, asking them if they have gerbils or rabbits at home. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a rabbit | Doesn't have a rabbit | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a gerbil | $18$ | $12$ | $30$ |\n| Doesn't have a gerbil | $6$ | $36$ | $42$ |\n| Totals | $24$ | $48$ | $72$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student at the school has a gerbil and also has a rabbit." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student at the school has a gerbil and also has a rabbit, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a gerbil\"** and the column **\"Has a rabbit\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a gerbil\"** and the column **\"Has a rabbit\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Has a rabbit | Doesn't have a rabbit | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a gerbil | $18$ | $12$ | $30$ |\n| Doesn't have a gerbil | $6$ | $36$ | $42$ |\n| Totals | $24$ | $48$ | $72$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(72),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{18}{72}=0.25.\n$$"}]}
